,title,link,text,language,family
0,Sharanawa language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharanawa_language,"January 1, 2026 - Sharanawa (Acre Arara) is a Panoan language of Peru. There are 200 Sharanawa (meaning 'good people') in Brazil, but only 3 speak the language. Its speakers call the language Arara.",Sharanahua,Panoan
1,La tradition des autobiographies chantées chez les Sharanahua (Amazonie occidentale),https://journals.openedition.org/bifea/3011,The Sharanahua (a Panoan people of Western Amazon) maintain a tradition of autobiographical songs. Two transcriptions and translations of such songs are presented here. A detailed analysis highlights two main problems.,Sharanahua,Panoan
2,Sharanahua | ILV,https://peru.sil.org/es/lengua_cultura/familia_linguistica_pano/sharanahua,"Como desean asimilarse a la cultura mestiza, los sharanahua han dejado muchas de sus costumbres, incluso las fiestas, la música y las danzas tradicionales. Aunque valoran su lengua materna, muchos hombres están aprendiendo a comunicarse en un castellano regional incipiente.",Sharanahua,Panoan
3,Sharanahua,https://dice.missouri.edu/assets/docs/pano/Sharanahua.pdf,"1.1 Name of society, language, and language family: Sharanahua “Good People” ETHNONYMS: Saranahua, Sharánahua1, Acre Arara,3 · They call themselves the Onicoin8. Their language belongs to the Central Branch of the Panoan Family1",Sharanahua,Panoan
4,"Sharanahua Language (MCD) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/mcd/,Sharanahua is a stable indigenous language of Peru. It belongs to the Panoan language family. The language is used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is taught as a subject of instruction in some schools.,Sharanahua,Panoan
5,Sharanahua,https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Sharanahua.htm,[ Home ] >> [ Chibchan ] >> [ Maipurean ] >> [ Cariban ] >> [ Quechuan ] >> [ Tupian ] >> [ Tucanoan ] >> [ Panoan] >> [ Other South American Languages ] >> [ Language isolate ] >> [ Unclassified languages ],Sharanahua,Panoan
6,Sharanahua | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/mcd/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..tYPSkFduTBWxz42KJ8jvr9RR7jw-_GfhyCpYZF41jWE,Sharanahua is a stable indigenous language of Peru. It belongs to the Panoan language family. The language is used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is taught as a subject of instruction in some schools.,Sharanahua,Panoan
7,Sharanahua | Encyclopedia.com,https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/sharanahua,"Many authorities consider the Marinahua to be also a part of the Sharanahua tribe. The population of this mixture of culturally similar and intermarrying peoples has been estimated at 1,350 to 1,850. Their language belongs to the Central Branch of the Panoan Family.",Sharanahua,Panoan
8,Category:Sharanahua language - Wiktionary,https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Category:Sharanahua_language,"Retrieved from ""https://en.wiktionary.org/w/index.php?title=Category:Sharanahua_language&oldid=61458405"" Categories: All languages · Panoan languages · Latin script languages · Languages of Peru · Languages of Brazil ·",Sharanahua,Panoan
9,"Sharanahua Language and the Sharanahua Indian Tribe (Sharanawa, Mastanahua, Marinawa, Chandinahua, Inonawa, Xaranáwa)",http://www.native-languages.org/sharanahua.htm,Sharanahua is a Panoan language of South America. Sharanahua is spoken by 400 people in eastern Peru.,Sharanahua,Panoan
